lass=\"equation\">(x + y)² = x² + y² + 2xy = 25 + 24 = 49,</p>\n  <p class=\"equation\">(x − y)² = x² + y² − 2xy = 25 − 24 = 1.</p>\n  <p>For a real number, a square equal to 49 has exactly the roots 7 and −7, and a square equal to 1 has exactly the roots 1 and −1. Thus</p>\n  <p class=\"equation\">x + y ∈ {7, −7}, &nbsp; x − y ∈ {1, −1}.</p>\n  <h2>2. Recover x and y for each sign choice</h2>\n  <p>Let s = x + y and d = x − y. Adding these equations gives 2x = s + d; subtracting the second from the first gives 2y = s − d. Dividing by the nonzero constant 2 yields</p>\n  <p class=\"equation\">x = (s + d)/2, &nbsp; y = (s − d)/2.</p>\n  <table>\n    <caption>All four choices for (s, d)</caption>\n    <thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">s</th><th scope=\"col\">d</th><th scope=\"col\">x</th><th scope=\"col\">y</th><th scope=\"col\">(x, y)</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>7</td><td>1</td><td>4</td><td>3</td><td>(4, 3)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>7</td><td>−1</td><td>3</td><td>4</td><td>(3, 4)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>−7</td><td>1</td><td>−3</td><td>−4</td><td>(−3, −4)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>−7</td><td>−1</td><td>−4</td><td>−3</td><td>(−4, −3)</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n  <h2>3. Verify the solutions and completeness</h2>\n  <p>For every sign choice above, s² = 49 and d² = 1. Substituting the formulas for x and y back into the original expressions gives</p>\n  <p class=\"equation\">x² + y² = [(s + d)² + (s − d)²]/4<br>= (s² + d²)/2 = (49 + 1)/2 = 25,</p>\n  <p class=\"equation\">xy = (s + d)(s − d)/4<br>= (s² − d²)/4 = (49 − 1)/4 = 12.</p>\n  <p>Hence all four listed pairs satisfy both equations. Every real solution must produce one of the four listed combinations of s and d, and each combination determines exactly one pair (x, y). Therefore there are no other real solutions.</p>\n</main>\n</body>\n</html>\n","tokensIn":5700,"tokensOut":17101,"runtimeMs":82694,"cacheHit":false,"costUsd":0,"ttftMs":82189,"tokensPerSec":33863.4,"rateKind":"decode","seq":5,"ts